==English==
[[File:Steamers.jpg|right|thumb|Two types of steamer (cooking appliance)]]

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|steam|er|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈstiːmə(ɹ)/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈstimɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|iːmə(ɹ)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|cookware|lang=en}} A [[cook]]ing [[appliance]] that cooks by steaming.
# {{rft-sense|lang=en}} A [[vessel]] in which articles are subjected to the action of steam, as in washing, and in various processes of manufacture.
# {{sense|steamship}} A vessel propelled by steam; a [[steamship]] or [[steamboat]].
# A steam-powered road locomotive; a [[traction engine]].
# A [[wetsuit]] which has long [[sleeve]]s and long [[leg]]s.
# A dish of steamed [[clam]]s.
# Any species of the [[duck]] genus ''[[Tachyeres]]'', of which all four species occur in South America, and three are flightless.
# {{context|Australia|food|obsolete|lang=en}} A food made by cooking [[dice]]d meat very slowly in a tightly sealed pot, with a minimum of flavourings, allowing it to [[steam]] in its own juices; ''popular circa 1850 but apparently no longer so by the 1900s''.
#* {{ante|1864}} “Melville”, ''Australia'', quoted in '''1864''', Edward Abbott, ''The English and Australian Cookery Book: Cookery for the Many, as Well as for the ‘Upper Ten Thousand’'', London, in turn quoted in '''1998''', Colin Bannerman, et al., ''Acquired Tastes: Celebrating Australia′s Culinary History'', {{w|National Library of Australia}} (publisher), ISBN 0-642-10693-2, page 14,
#*: Of all the dishes ever brought to table, nothing equals that of the '''steamer'''.
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A steam [[fire engine]], a fire engine consisting of a steam boiler and engine, and pump which is driven by the engine, combined and mounted on wheels (Webster 1913).
# {{context|horse racing|lang=en}} A horse whose [[odds]] are decreasing (becoming shorter) because bettors are backing it.
# {{short for|steamer trunk|lang=en}}
# {{context|UK|crime|slang|lang=en}} Member of a youth [[gang]] who engages in [[robbing]] and [[escaping]] as a large group.
# {{context|UK|sex|slang|lang=en}} [[oral sex|Oral sex]] performed on a man.
# {{context|UK|slang|lang=en}} A [[homosexual]] man with a preference for [[passive]] partners.
# {{context|UK|crime|slang|lang=en}} A [[prostitute]]'s [[client]].
# {{context|US|gambling|slang|lang=en}} A [[gambler]] who increases a [[wager]] after losing.
# {{context|UK|Scotland|slang|lang=en}} A [[drinking]] [[session]].
# A [[babycino]] (frothy milk drink).

